Two ways a clip outlived what it showed once a VNAS turn ended.

A step's clip loops while its stage lasts, and the card stops the loop when
the stage goes idle if the clip is a stage's or the step clip the card last
saw. A step that came while the card was paused, locked or asleep was not
seen, but the clip came back when the pause ended (or after a click or a
drag), and when the turn then ended without a success or an error the clip
looped until the next turn. The card now also stops a looping event clip:
every clip a step asks for is an event, and the other events play once.

When the stage goes idle and the page asks for a clip in the same moment,
as the curtsy when the member stops a turn, the step's clip stops looping
and stays in front while the curtsy loads. If it ended during that load,
its end moved the chain on (to idle), which replaced the curtsy before it
showed. The end of the clip in front is now held back while a switch loads:
the switch's clip takes over once loaded, and a switch whose clip fails to
fetch or to decode is released and hands the held end on.

